Below is a minimal fix to the Ruby grammar so that single-quoted (non-interpolated) heredocs as well as “<<-FOO.chomp” style openers are recognized and the ending delimiter picked up correctly.

### What’s wrong

In the existing `ruby.js` grammar the heredoc variant is written as:

```js
{ // heredocs
  begin: /<<[-~]?'?(\w+)\n(?:[^\n]*\n)*?\s*\1\b/,
  returnBegin: true,
  contains: [
    { begin: /<<[-~]?'?/ },
    hljs.END_SAME_AS_BEGIN({
      begin: /(\w+)/,
      end: /(\w+)/,
      contains: [
        hljs.BACKSLASH_ESCAPE,
        SUBST
      ]
    })
  ]
}
```

This only allows an *optional* leading single-quote before the delimiter name (so it matches `<<-'MESSAGE…`) but it never consumes the trailing single-quote, nor does it allow a method call like `.chomp` on the opener. As a result the opener regex never matches those cases and the highlighter never sees it as a string.

### The fix

1. In the **outer** `begin` regex, allow an optional trailing quote and an optional “`.`+word” method call before the newline.
2. Leave the inner `END_SAME_AS_BEGIN` alone (it will still find the unquoted closing delimiter on its own).

